Z||000035||BN Boomer||10-19-2001||12:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<P>con's want to turn back the clock and have no respect for others.<BR>liberals are the driving force of progress<BR>con's backward views are regressive and repressive and are allways anti-progressive.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I fail to see how socialism can be called progressive. It hasn't been shown to be a very effective form of government anywhere else. I don't like the idea of giving the government ever increasing amounts of my hrd earned income so they can spend as they see fit and only give me what they think necessary. Ever notice how countries that embrace socialism to any significant degree never seem to have an economy as vibrant as the United States or a similar standard of living?<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>no good ever comes from con's world views only wars and death and repression<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hmmmmm..... Okay. How convenient of you to forget to mention that it was Democrat presidents who got us into WWII, Korea, and radically escalated our presence in Vietnam.<P>And personally, I consider the financial burden of socialism along with it's nannying people from cradle to grave to be pretty d*mn repressive in it's own right. There must be some reason so many of the world's people want to come to America to enjoy the fruits of freedom. Problem is, success here requires effort, which in my opinion is an anathema to the weak, lazy, unmotivated, and outright leaches who want nothing but to lie about under the umbrella of socialism and allow it provide it's meager sustenace.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>CRUSEMISSiLS mayhave missed but clinton did shoot at him<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Unfortunately, given the backdrop of revelations and legal repercussions of Clinton's extramarrital affairs at the time, great legitimacy was given to the notion that he was simply trying to draw attention away from his own scandals. The action also appeared to be rather hasty to me, wholly unlike our current course in Afghanistan, and it's total lack of success made America appear futile and impotent, probaly only further emboldening the terrorists.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>emember " itis the economy stupid " as why bush lost in 92??? guess not. con's hate for billy-bob clinton over rides the money he made them.and only after bush raked in record bribes and looked like a winner did the market start downhill in 3-2000 slide<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Rubbish. The only thing for which Clinton can take any real direct credit about the remarkable boom of the 90's was not being a typical "progressive" and trying to tax the living hell out of it. Frankly, I don't know why so many people subscribe to the idea that the President is such a great force in shifting the economy anyhow. It is going to ebb and tide on it's own accord regardless of who is in office, unless a hard core "progressive" somehow gets elected and decides to seize it all and dole out only what he decides that each of us needs. Then it will only go one way, down the toilet, just like every other economy that has had socialism foisted upon it.<P>And I suppose next you are going to tell us that Gore's visit to the Buddhist temple really was just a social call, right? I think the Democrats set a few records in accepting "bribes" themselves. By the way, your heroes the Clintons don't seem to be doing to badly themselves. Think Hillary can give us any advice on trading cattle futures or how to get an 8 million dollar advance to write your memoirs after 8 years of claiming that you can't recall anything? Must be nice to be so "progressive". Interesting how you can simply overlook 8 years of scandal. <BR>||||reg||1
