Z||000041||EdsB52||10-19-2001||02:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR>[B][B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"I'm not busting his balls just for grammar."<P>Maybe not exclusively for his grammar, but the 'dictionary' remark was there.<P>"His posts are largely unintelligible due to his haphazard way of writing."<P>I know what you mean, and it would be hypocritical of me to defame you for being critical of anyone for poor grammar and syntax, as I'm a stickler as well.<P>"I'm not claiming to be perfect, nor am I claiming that my views are right and his are wrong."<P>Don't forget about the glass house thing! I make errors from time to time, but if I'm busting someone's balls, I'm sure to be grammatically correct during that post.<P>"I was simply stating that people will take him more seriously if he takes the time to word his posts so people can understand him."<P>True, but it's entirely possible that he has great ideas while being sloppy or even somewhat illiterate. This is why our political system is so hosed up; because the people that spend the most time becoming popular and believable aren't the thinkers, they're the politicians. How many Presidents have had PhD’s? How many Reagan’s’, slick willies, and GW's (low 2 GPA) are there compared to more, well read leaders like Benjamin Franklins? <P>"I disagree with a lot you have to say, but your posts are more clearly worded.  At least I have a better idea of what you mean, instead of having to guess and try to pull some meaning out of a chaotic post."<P>And this is where you drive down the 'ad hominem' road. But don't worry; you're with many of the forum members. Now I've attacked many members here, but I ultimately attack the content of their posts, or lack there of, as opposed to dismissing what they have to say based on my dislike of their character. And at times I've agreed with people I don't like; I can do this while still maintaining my disdain for them. So is it that you disagree with me, or that it's fashionable to disagree so you jump on board the 'contest Ed' bandwagon? It's amazing to read people apologize for the rare times they agree with me. Again, many members share your company with this ad hominem policy.<P>"As far as his views being factually viable, that's debatable.  His views are so extreme as to hinder their believability."<P>Ya, he's an extremist. And I can see your point, however I tend to agree with some of what he writes (no apologies).<P>"There are few absolutes in life, and especially in politics.  It's all shades of gray and not simply black and white."<P>Brilliant point. See, now you're distancing yourself from the rest of the forum with this remark. I like to say the ends that comprise 10% of the continuum on both sides are black and white, while the 80% in the middle is gray. The more education I receive, the larger the gray area has grown. I've had this debate with forum members and they tend to subscribe to a smaller gray margin. <P>"There are several factual mistakes in his posts.  One of which Orief pointed out by mentioning that Bush Sr. couldn't go after Saddam himself once Iraq was out of Kuwait because the UN wouldn't let us.  It was a coalition of forces, not the US vs. Iraq.  We had to abide by what the coalition wanted to acheive, not just what US interests were."<P>Well, I didn't read Orief's post, but going off your extrapolation, you've made several assumptions. You might be right, but you still made assumptions. First, you assume that Bush Sr wanted to go after Sadam. Then you assume the reports that the media’s reporting that the UN wouldn't allow us to peruse any further was true. You assume the US wouldn't have originally proceeded without the blessing of the UN forces, which I believe is false. And finally you count as accurate the media’s translation of data as accurate and not skewed or impartial. Do you think the US would have proceeded without the UN's blessing? I do, just as they would right now. The blessing is just in case something goes horribly wrong; we can share the blame (IMO). <P>"I think it was a mistake leaving Saddam in power, but it would have ignited an international powder keg to go after him when the rest of the coalition is saying stop."<P>I disagree. I think we left Iraq alone because election time was approaching and Bush smelled like a rose. It was a very opportune time to quit for GW.<P>"And on your last point, I fail to see how it could matter whether or not I've met Ray in person.  No, I haven't.  All the more reason to word his posts more clearly.  If the post is the only communication people have with you, then that's the only way you have to influence them or build a reputation."<P>Do you recall writing, "ray b,<BR>Man, you gotta learn to type, talk, and think.” <P>If you require he learn to talk, then you must have heard him speak. His writing isn’t great, but for all we know, he could be the most articulate speaker to walk the earth. Einstein was a genius, yet a social buffoon through what I’ve read. You can’t always correlate like attributes. Another error you made was that you contrived the idea I was asking if you ever met with Ray. I never asked that, I asked, “Besides, have you ever spoken with Ray?” You could have spoken with Ray without ever having met Ray. Do you see how people read what others write and translate it into terms they more readily identify with? Most people do this, through my experiences and experiments I’ve read, but it’s fallacious. I hope you don’t perceive me breaking down your writings as taking them out of context. If you do, please politely correct me, but I believe I’ve fairly broken down your assertions and kept them in context.||||reg||1
Z||000042||EdsB52||10-19-2001||02:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B> Well said.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>See what I mean by plenty of company on that bandwagon? LZeitgeist didn't read the posts carefully enough to remark, but he did anyway. I never asked if you met with Ray, I simply asked if you've spoken with him. People read what they want, not necessarily what is written.<BR>||||reg||1
